  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of rain today and Sunday.\n\nKey word is periods. It will not rain the entire time nor\neverywhere at the same time this weekend. Surface front will\nmove from north to south through the forecast area today with\nband of frontal rain with embedded heavier showers. Primary\nfocus for the steadier rainfall this morning will be across the\nHighway 81 and 460 corridors with amounts of 0.25 to 0.75. \nFront will then sag south into the Carolinas later in the day \nwith daytime heating resulting in the activity becoming more \nshowery in nature and more hit/miss with respect to rainfall \nalong and south of the VA/NC border...amounts 0.25 or less, \nalthough can't rule out an isolated thunderstorm producing more.\n\nBy tonight the front will be south of the forecast area with\nlull in the precipitation production, but remaining overcast. A\nwave of low pressure is expected to move east along the front\nearly Sunday and this may bring a second round of rain and drizzle\nacross parts of the forecast area for Sunday, favoring the \nsouthern and eastern CWA or areas along and east of the Blue\nRidge for the steadier rainfall...QPF of 0.10 to 0.25.\n\nWhen overlapping the two rain forecast footprints, today and \nSunday, there will likely be some folks that get little or no \nmeaningful rainfall...basically the same area that has been \ndrought stricken for months...the piedmont. The outlook for next\nweek is bleak with another string of dry days Monday through \nSaturday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Seasonally cool air arrives today and persists \nthrough next week... no more 85 to near 90 degree weather. \n\nPassage of a cold front today will introduce cooler\ntemperatures. The cool down will be more in line with where we \nshould be for October. Dewpoints will also trend lower, so the \nair will have a chill to it at night. Once we clear out behind \nthe front Monday, look for a broader swing in daytime vs \nnighttime temperatures...60s and 70s during the day vs 40s at \nnight, with potential to dip into the 30s in the mountain \nvalleys which may promote some patchy frost.\n\nIn general looking at a 5 day stretch of pleasant fall weather \nstarting Monday. Dewpoints should begin to creep back up next\nweekend ahead of another front. Until then the forecast looks\ndry and seasonal for the work week.",
